{"result": {"response": {"id": "resp_0a5847010e95cc300169bb2d0320848191938230ba0e6f4267", "created": 1773874435, "model": "gpt-5.2-2025-12-11", "object": "chat.completion", "system_fingerprint": null, "choices": [{"finish_reason": "stop", "index": 0, "message": {"content": "{\"title\":\"AML Diagnosis, Accurate Testing, and Treatment/Recovery Monitoring\",\"summary\":\"This community centers on the clinical workflow for acute myeloid leukemia (AML): establishing an AML diagnosis through identification of myeloid blasts (with common threshold-based criteria), then managing AML treatment and the recovery period through frequent, repeated testing—especially blood testing—to track treatment response, blood counts, and organ function. A dedicated book section (Chapter 4: AML) is positioned within the NCCN Guidelines for Patients book, framing how the testing-to-diagnosis-to-treatment continuum is described in the source material. Overall, the entities are linked as a care pathway in which accurate testing enables diagnosis, and ongoing blood tests support treatment decisions and recovery monitoring.\",\"findings\":[{\"summary\":\"AML diagnosis is anchored on detecting myeloid blasts, often using a threshold framework.\",\"explanation\":\"The community describes AML diagnosis as being based on the presence and amount of myeloid blasts in bone marrow or peripheral blood. This establishes myeloid blasts as the central diagnostic element connecting the diagnostic concept to a measurable cellular finding. [Data: Entities (1724,2937); Relationships (5516,2852)]\\n\\nThe material also notes that diagnostic frameworks commonly use a marrow blast threshold (often around 20%) as a defining criterion, while acknowledging that certain defining genetic/chromosomal abnormalities can affect how strictly that percentage criterion is applied. This highlights that diagnosis can be both morphology-driven (blast percentage) and, in some cases, informed by additional defining abnormalities. [