re continuum. [Data: Entities (1706); Relationships (2846)]\\n\\nBecause the relationship explicitly includes both treatment and recovery, it reinforces that blood testing remains central beyond initial therapy, serving as a routine mechanism for tracking patient status and guiding decisions over time. [Data: Relationships (2846)]\"},{\"summary\":\"Recovery is explicitly defined as a period where testing—particularly blood testing—continues to monitor status and organ function.\",\"explanation\":\"AML recovery is described as the period following or alongside treatment during which testing continues to monitor treatment results, blood counts, and organ function. This makes recovery a monitored clinical phase rather than a passive interval. [Data: Entities (2938)]\\n\\nA specific relationship further ties AML recovery to blood testing, stating that blood tests are performed during recovery to monitor ongoing status and organ health. This provides a concrete example of how monitoring persists after or alongside active treatment. [Data: Entities (2938); Relationships (5540)]\"},{\"summary\":\"The content is framed within a structured patient-guideline source: Chapter 4: AML in the NCCN Guidelines for Patients book.\",\"explanation\":\"A dedicated entity, Chapter 4: AML, is characterized as focusing specifically on AML treatment, indicating that the community’s concepts are organized within a focused chapter rather than dispersed references. [Data: Entities (1718)]\\n\\nChapter 4: AML is identified as part of the NCCN Guidelines for Patients book, providing context for how the information is packaged and delivered (i.e., as patient-facing guideline material). [Data: Entities (1718); Relationships (5576)]\"},{\"summary\":\"Overall community structure forms a care pathway: testing → diagnosis (myeloid blasts) → treatment → recovery monitoring (blood tests).\",\"explanation\":\"The relationships in this community connect accurate testing to AML diagnosis, and AML diagnosis